      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x-3y}\n$$\n\nWork on an open domain where all displayed expressions are defined and the dependent-variable factor is nonzero. Choose an equivalent equation in the form $A(y)\\, \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=B(x)$, with a multiplier depending only on $y$ and a right-hand side depending only on $x$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Expose the independent-variable and dependent-variable factors:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x-3y}={e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}}.\n$$\n\nMultiply both sides by the reciprocal of the dependent-variable factor. On the stated domain, this gives\n\n$$\n{e}^{3y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative multiplier depends only on $y$ and the right-hand side only on $x$.",
